# LINE ENDINGS — this repo is LF, on every contributor's platform.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for Windows default) checks the
# repo out with CRLF. A CRLF shell script does not warn, it fails to PARSE
# (`syntax error near unexpected token $'{\r'`), which takes out
# `scripts/*.sh` and every CI step that runs them; CRLF also makes a Docker
# image's entrypoint unrunnable and shows every line of a file as changed the
# first time a Linux contributor touches it.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalizes to LF in the index AND on checkout, regardless
# of the contributor's core.autocrlf setting.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Binaries must never be newline-normalized.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# Lockfiles are generated artifacts — keep them out of diffs.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff
uv.lock        -diff
mise.lock      -diff

# CHANGELOG is append-only: every PR adds bullets under `### [Unreleased]`, so
# concurrent PRs touch the same lines and collide. The built-in `union` merge
# driver resolves this by keeping BOTH sides' added lines instead of writing
# conflict markers — honored by local merge/rebase and by GitHub's merge button.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
